loading Sitronix ST7735 display

| This is code to write to the ST7735 display on the Sipeed Longan Nano
| board.
|
| Here is a published schematic of the board:
|
| https://doc.nucleisys.com/nuclei_sdk/_images/sipeed_longan_nano_schematic.png
|
| Of interest to us here are the connections to the display:
|
| MCU    LCD    Function
| ~~~~~  ~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
| PA5    SCL    SPI1_SCK  (NOTE: GigaDevice calls this SPI0!)
| PA7    SDA    SPI2_MOSI
| PB0    RS     Command/data: 0=command, 1=data
| PB1    RST    reset
| PB2    CS     chip select
|
| According to the schematic, the backlight voltage is sourced from a 2.8V
| regulator.

__meta
hex

| We need a basic kernel, since we are going to write this in Forth.
.target. .contains @ .not .if  error" ST7735 code requires a Forth kernel."  .then

| Copied this code from chat-asm. It should really be in its own file.
|
| On older chips - eg F103/F105 - and on the new GigaDevice GD32VF103! -
| the GPIO configuration is somewhat different.
|
| GPIOx_CRL and _CRH have 4 bits per GPIO pin: CNF<2>:MODE<2>
|
| Here are the tables of their meanings:
|
| MODE  Meaning
|  00   Input
|  01   Output; Maximum speed 10 MHz
|  10   Output; Maximum speed 2 MHz
|  11   Output; Maximum speed 50 MHz
|
| If MODE = 00:
| CNF   Meaning
|  00   Analog
|  01   Input floating
|  10   Input pull-up / pull-down
|  11   Reserved
|
| If MODE != 00:
| CNF   Meaning
|  00   Output, general purpose, push-pull
|  01   Output, general purpose, open-drain
|  10   Output, alternate function, push-pull
|  11   Output, alternate function, open-drain
|
| If MODE = 00 (input) and CNF = 10 (pull-up/pull-down), the GPIOx_ODR bit
| corresponding to the pin determines the direction of the pull-up/-down:
|
| ODR
|  0   pull-down
|  1   pull-up

__host

( CNF bits, MODE=0)
0 equ gpio-in-analog
4 equ gpio-in-floating
8 equ gpio-in-pulled

( CNF bits, MODE=1,2,3)
4 equ gpio-open-drain
8 equ gpio-alternate-function

( MODE bits.)
1 equ gpio-out-10m
2 equ gpio-out-2m
3 equ gpio-out-50m

__meta

| GPIO registers have to be accessed by word only.

| Set and clear port B bits.
: bit/  ( bit)  GPIOB_BSRR ! ;      | set bit
: bit\  ( bit)  #16 <<  bit/ ;      | clear bit

: disp-init
   [ 1 2 << ( IOPA_EN)
     1 3 << ( IOPB_EN) +
   1 #12 << ( SPI1_EN) + #] RCC_APB2ENR set!

   | PB0/1/2 are active low outputs. Set them to de-asserted before
   | enabling them as outputs.
   7 bit/

   | Set up PB0/1/2 as GPIO outputs, 10M toggle freq.
   GPIOB_CRL dup @
                    0fff      bic   | 4 bits per pin
      [ gpio-out-10m 111 * #] or
   swap !

   | Assign PA5/7 to SPI1.
   | We want to run the SPI at 12M, so set slew rate to 50M.
   GPIOA_CRL dup @
                                 f0f0_0000      bic
      [ gpio-alternate-function
        gpio-out-50m +           1010_0000 * #] or
   swap !

   | Set up SPI peripheral.
   %0011_01_001_100 ( 8bit data, SSM=1, SSI=1, SPE=1, PCLK/4, MSTR=1, CPOL,CPHA=00)
   | %0011_00_001_100 ( 8bit data, SSM=1, SSI=1, SPE=0, PCLK/4, MSTR=1, CPOL,CPHA=00)
   | 0
   SPI1_CR1
      0 over !  ( reset and disable)
      | tuck !  ( config)
           !  ( config and enable) ;

( Command/data select on PB0. Command=0, data=1.)
: cmd/  ( de-assert command mode)  1 bit/ ;
: cmd\  ( assert command mode)     1 bit\ ;

( /RST on PB1.)
: rst/  ( de-assert /RST)   2 bit/ ;
: rst\  ( assert /RST)      2 bit\ ;

( /CS on PB2.)
: cs/  ( de-assert /CS)     4 bit/ ;
: cs\  ( assert /CS)        4 bit\ ;

| NOTE: Accesses to SPI registers must be halfword or word; no byte access!

| Wait until status bit set or reset.
: until-set     ( mask)  begin  SPI1_SR @  over and     until  drop ;
: until-clear   ( mask)  begin  SPI1_SR @  over and  0= until  drop ;

| Send a byte over SPI. "to spi"
| >s  ( b)   SPI1_DR ! ;   | Forth runs too slowly to cause SPI overrun!
code >s  ( b)
   SPI1_DR w lui   SPI1_DR w top sw   top pop1   next  ;c

| >lohi  ( w - lo hi)  dup 0ff and  swap 8 u>> ;
code >lohi  ( w - lo hi)
   0ff top w andi  w push1  top 8 top srli  next  ;c

| Send 16-bit big-endian value. >sw is "to spi word".
: >sw  ( w)  >lohi >s >s ;

| Wait until transmit complete.
: ?bsy  [ 1 7 << ( BSY) #] until-clear ;

| Sitronix ST7735 commands of interest.
|
| Hex   Data bytes         Description
| ~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
| 10    --                 Sleep in
| 11    --                 Sleep out
| 28    --                 Display off
| 29    --                 Display on
| 2a    X start, X end     Column address set (CASET)
| 2b    Y start, Y end     Row address set (RASET)
| 2c    pixels             Memory write (RAMWR)
| 36    bits               Memory access control (MADCTL)
| 3a    bits               Interface pixel format (COLMOD)
|
| For CASET and RASET the start and end addresses are sent as 16-bit
| big-endian values.
|
| For MADCTL the bits are as follows:
|
|   MY MX MV ML RGB MH - -
|
|  MY : Mirror y
|  MX : Mirror x
|  MV : swap x and y
| RGB : order of pixel components
|
| ML and MH are the directions that the hardware panel is scanned; we
| ignore these.
|
| For the ST7735 on the Longan Nano, we need to set MV so we can use the
| display in "landscape" mode; we set MX if we want the origin in the upper
| left corner - otherwise it's in the lower left; and if we send the pixel
| components in the order R G B we need to set the RGB bit.
|
| COLMOD bits: - - - - - IFPF2 IFPF1 IFPF0
| Pixel formats: 011  12 bits (4/4/4)
|                101  16 bits (5/6/5)
|                110  18 bits (6/6/6)
| Device resets to 18 bits, so we probably don't need to set it.
|
| Pixel formats for writing to memory:
| 12 bits:  rrrr_gggg bbbb_rrrr gggg_bbbb  (1.5 bytes/pixel)
| 16 bits:  rrrr_rggg gggb_bbbb            (2 bytes/pixel)
| 18 bits:  rrrr_rr00 gggg_gg00 bbbb_bb00  (3 bytes/pixel)
|
| Serial data is sent MSB first.

| At the start of each command byte, pulse CS high to reset the serial
| interface; then set command mode, send command byte, wait for
| transmission to end, and set data mode.

: cmd  ( cmd)  cs/ cs\  cmd\  >s ?bsy  cmd/ ;

| For simple commands with no parameters.
meta: cmd0  ( cmd)  constant  does> @ cmd ;

10 cmd0 +sleep    | "sleep in"
11 cmd0 -sleep    | "sleep out"

20 cmd0 -invert
21 cmd0 +invert

28 cmd0 -display  | display off
29 cmd0 +display  | display on

: caset  ( end start)  2a cmd  >sw >sw ;    | Column address set (CASET)
: raset  ( end start)  2b cmd  >sw >sw ;    | Row address set (RASET)

2c cmd0 ramwr  ( write to display ram)

: madctl  ( bits)  36 cmd  >s ;

decimal

| Write a pixel.
| px  ( rgb)  >lohi  ( b rg) >sw >s ;

| We play fast and loose with the truth here - we are not masking off the
| remaining high bits. Since the only use for this word is sending bytes
| via SPI, and the SPI engine will ignore the high bits, we are ok.

code >bgr  ( rgb - b g r)
   top w mv
   top  8   x srli
   top 16 top srli   w x push2   next  ;c

: px  ( rgb)  >bgr  >s >s >s ;

| Include the printable ASCII chars (32 to 126) from OpenBSD's Spleen 8x16
| font.

meta: font
   create  ( 'font b/glyph #glyphs )
      over ,  *  push  here image+ r@ cmove  pop allot
   does> @+ swap ( char 'glyphs b/glyph)  rot 32 - * + ;

spleen-8x16
   16 127 32 - ( skip delete char)  font spleen

variable fg    | foreground color for drawing

| Test the sign bit. If set, copy fg to px; otherwise px is 0. Shift bits
| left one bit.

code 1bit  ( fg bits - fg bits' px)
   top w mv   0 top li   w 0< if   0 sp top s.lw   then   w 1 w slli
   begin   w push1   next  ;c

| This is a special word for fetching the next row of 8 bits. Note that it
| puts the value on top rather than the incremented address, unlike, say @+
| It also shifts the value up so that its MSB is in the sign bit.

code row@+  ( a - a+1 bits)
   top w mv   0 w top lbu   top 24 top slli   1 w w addi   again  ;c

code 2drop   top w pop2   next  ;c

: 8bits  ( a - a+1)
   | row@+  fg @  swap  ( a+1 fg bits)  8 for  2dup 0< and px  2*  next  2drop ;
   row@+  fg @  swap  ( a+1 fg bits)  8 for  1bit px  next  2drop ;

: glyph  ( char)
   spleen  ramwr  16 for  8bits  next  drop ;

| Goto text row n, assuming an Nx16 font.
: row  ( n)  4 << ( *16)  26 +  dup 15 + swap  raset ;

| Goto text col n, assuming an 8xM font.
: col  ( n)  3 << ( *8)  1+  dup 7 + swap  caset ;

variable curcol
variable currow

: cr   currow @  1+ dup row  currow !
                           0 curcol ! ;

: locate  curcol @  dup col 1+  curcol ! ;
: ?cr     curcol @  20 = if  cr  then ;

variable 'wrap
: wrap    ['] ?cr   'wrap ! ;
: nowrap  ['] nope  'wrap ! ;

: emit   ( char)  locate  glyph  'wrap @execute ;

code c@+  ( a - b a+1)  0 top w lbu   w push1   1 top top addi   next  ;c

: count  ( a" - a u)  c@+ swap ;
: type   ( a u)  for  count emit  next  drop ;
: atype ( a")  count type ;

| NOTE: a" is the address of the count byte.
: (")  ( - a")  r> dup  c@+ + 3+ -4 and  >r ; [r]

| Compile a string into a target word. At runtime, (") will push the
| address of the count byte, which precedes the string.

tc: "
   \t (") compile,
   char " parse  push  r@ c,  here image+ r@ cmove  pop allot align ;

| Display initialization.

| Panel goes from (1,26) to (160,105), assuming MV (swap x and y) set.

: clear
   160 1 caset  105 26 raset  ramwr  [ 160 80 * #] for  0 px  next
   -1 currow !  cr ;

: +disp   disp-init  "68 madctl  clear  +invert  -sleep  +display ;

: demo
   +disp  "ffff00 ( yellow) fg !  nowrap
       " Brought to you by" atype
   cr  " OpenBSD's spleen8x16"  atype
   cr  " font and muforth!" atype
   cr cr  " 2026/09/01" atype ;

: demo2  "ff00ff ( magenta) fg !  clear  wrap  127 32 do  i emit  loop ;
